From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PAL: the CJK decoders' tables without ICU's converters

WebCore's own CJK codecs (TextCodecCJK: Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, ISO-2022-JP,
EUC-KR, Big5, GBK and gb18030) look up the Encoding Standard's indexes.
WebKit builds five of those indexes at the first use by running ICU's
EUC-JP, Big-5, windows-949 and gb18030 converters over every pointer, and
keeps the Encoding Standard's own tables only in debug builds, to compare.

The ICU data the engine carries (icu/data-keep.lst) has none of those
converters. ucnv_open() fails, every lookup reads an unset character, and
the JIS X 0208 builder writes 8,836 entries into its 7,724: the first
Shift_JIS, EUC-JP or ISO-2022-JP text ends the engine in a libstdc++
assertion (std::array::operator[]); Big5, EUC-KR and GBK text decodes to
rubbish. Any page can do it, with its charset or a TextDecoder.

The indexes are now the Encoding Standard's tables, in every build: the
tables WebKit already has for its debug checks (jis0208, jis0212, big5,
euc-kr and gb18030, 319,956 bytes of constant data). ICU is no longer
called for them, which also moves us on in PLAN.md step 7 (ICU out).
The gb18030 four-byte decoder returns an error, not an out-of-range read,
for a pointer below the first range.

---
diff --git a/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/text/EncodingTables.cpp b/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/text/EncodingTables.cpp
--- a/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/text/EncodingTables.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/text/EncodingTables.cpp
@@ -26,24 +26,12 @@
